          "question": "No chest pain at all, but I do get a bit puffed walking up the hill lately. Is that just age?",
          "answer": "The guidance I can search does not say whether breathlessness on hills is \"just age,\" so I can't answer that question directly. Only a clinician who examines you can tell you why it is happening.\n\nTwo points from the retrieved guidance are worth knowing:\n\n- **Breathlessness can be a warning sign on its own.** The emergency guidance on chest pain (red-flag-chest-pain) says that breathlessness alone, without chest pain, is a recognized way a heart attack can present. It says this is more common in older adults. It also says the absence of chest pain is not reassuring.\n- **Breathlessness that is out of proportion to effort should be checked.** The physical activity guidance (lifestyle-hypertension-activity) says that breathlessness disproportionate to effort should be assessed by a medical professional before starting vigorous activity.\n\nThe triage check did not flag an emergency from your description. It also cannot clear you, because it only covers a fixed list of patterns.\n\n**Seek emergency care now** if you have breathlessness at rest or that comes on suddenly, chest pain with breathlessness, blue or grey lips or fingertips, fainting, or confusion (severe-breathing-difficulty guidance).\n\n**Otherwise, book a non-urgent appointment soon** and mention the change: new breathlessness on hills, how long it has been going on, and whether it is getting worse. A clinician can decide what tests, if any, are needed.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
